WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.005 --> 00:00:11.130
JAMIE KINNEY: Hi, everyone.

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.340
I'd like to welcome
you to INF300.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.050
And thank you for
joining us today

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:20.260
for this Cloud Next session,
exploring GCE VM families.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:22.180
Today, we're going to
start by giving you

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.410
an overview of the
GCE VM families.

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.410
And then we're going to
have a deep dive looking

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.470
at each of these VM families
to see how they're tailored

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.750
to meet the needs of
individual workloads,

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:33.520
looking at our
general-purpose VM

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:38.500
families, compute-intensive VM
families, memory-optimized VMs,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:41.050
and then finally, options for
running accelerated workloads

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:43.953
on Google Compute Engine.

00:00:43.953 --> 00:00:45.620
I'd like to start by
introducing myself.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:46.580
I'm Jamie McKinney.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:47.690
I'm a product manager.

00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:50.825
And I am responsible for
the Tau T2D VM family.

00:00:50.825 --> 00:00:52.700
I joined Google a little
bit over three years

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:55.880
ago initially as a cloud
solutions architect,

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.310
focusing on both scientific
and technical computing,

00:00:58.310 --> 00:01:00.500
and also working with some
of Google Cloud's largest

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:05.620
customers as they migrated their
cloud-native workloads to GCE.

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.560
Subra, would you like
to introduce yourself?

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:07.640
SUBRA CHANDRAMOULI: Sure.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:08.330
Thanks, Jamie.

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:09.800
Hey, my name is
Subra Chandramouli.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.470
I'm also a product
manager at Google Cloud,

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:15.530
covering Google Compute
Engine products.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.800
My background has been primarily
in the semiconductor industry,

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.730
focused on processors
and FPGA accelerators

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:25.340
for data center
markets, including--

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:27.520
amongst others.

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.440
JAMIE KINNEY: Thanks, Subra.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:29.940
OK.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.315
Just to kick things
off, I'd like

00:01:31.315 --> 00:01:33.660
to start with an
overview of what GCE is.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.820
So GCE, or Google
Compute Engine,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.860
is Google Cloud's
managed service

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:40.320
for offering virtual
machines as a service.

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.440
With GCE, you can
easily choose a VM

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.900
that meets your needs,
either selecting

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:50.550
one of our predefined shapes
or custom-defining your own.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.440
Following our recommendations,
if you'd like,

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.870
or if you have the
exact specifications,

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.720
you can dial in that VM to
precisely meet your needs.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.920
GCE also takes care of a lot
of the operational overhead

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.560
of running applications,
offering technologies

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.770
like live migration,
for example, which

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.860
will allow us to easily
move your virtual machine

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.980
to a server while we perform
maintenance on a machine that

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.110
might need some assistance.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:18.415
We can also give you
a number of mechanisms

00:02:18.415 --> 00:02:20.790
to reduce the cost of running
your workloads when you run

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.770
on top of Google Compute
Engine with features

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.830
like sustained use discounts
that automatically provide

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.110
discounts for
long-running applications.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.630
Or if you'd like to get
even deeper cost savings,

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:34.080
you can look at our one-year
and three-year committed use

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.400
discounts.

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.970
Today, our focus is going
to be on GCE VM families

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:41.460
and how to choose the right
family for the needs of a given

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.605
workload.

00:02:43.605 --> 00:02:45.230
Just to provide a
little bit of history

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.990
on Google Compute Engine, we
initially launched the service

00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:51.260
back in June of 2012.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.120
And at that time,
Google Compute Engine

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:55.340
offered just a single
type of virtual machine.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.210
And over the past nine years,
on top of that single VM family,

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.280
we also built a
number of features,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:04.040
things like live
migration, the ability

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.470
to attach persistent
disk, and other features

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.530
that make those virtual machines
even more capable and powerful

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:12.530
and a better fit for your
applications and their needs.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:14.567
In recent years, we've
expanded the number

00:03:14.567 --> 00:03:17.150
of virtual machine families that
are offered by Google Compute

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:20.090
Engine to include
compute-optimized,

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:23.840
memory-optimized, accelerated
workload-optimized VMs,

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.320
and even more
general-purpose VM families.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.030
So today, we're going
to look into each

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:32.960
of these young families and
see how they're best applied.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:35.210
Starting with a map to
help you orient yourself

00:03:35.210 --> 00:03:37.700
around the different VM
families that we offer,

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:40.760
we generally divide these into
two high-level categories,

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:45.080
general-purpose VMs and
workload-optimized VM families.

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:48.840
On the general purpose side,
we have the cost-optimized VM

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:49.380
families.

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:50.480
This includes E2.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:52.790
And these are our VM
families that are designed

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:56.180
to minimize the price per vCPU.

00:03:56.180 --> 00:03:58.340
We also offer a
balanced VM family

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.190
that gives you access to
all of GCE's features--

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:05.240
the flexibility to attach
local SSDs, to attach

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.610
any of our persistent
disk variants,

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.190
access to the
fastest networking,

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:13.940
and the ability to dial
in the custom VM shapes.

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.130
For customers that are
running scale-out workloads

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:17.660
for a wide range
of applications,

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:20.480
be it web serving
application serving

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.180
or even high-throughput
computing workloads,

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.360
we've introduced a new category
of VM families designed

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:30.200
to meet those needs, currently
including the Tau T2D VM

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:33.180
family that you'll be
hearing more from Subra.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:34.980
On the other side
of this map, you

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.230
see the workload-optimized
VM families.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.360
These are VM families
that are specifically

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.650
tailored to meet the needs
of a possibly niche or a very

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.990
well-defined set
of applications.

00:04:45.990 --> 00:04:48.030
This could include the
compute-optimized VM

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.060
family that's really well suited
for high-performance computing,

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.470
game servers, and other
compute-intensive applications;

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:58.080
memory-optimized VMs for
vertically-scaled applications

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.180
that need access to
large amounts of memory

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.890
and balanced compute
to go with it;

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:05.088
and accelerator-optimized
VMs with TPUs and GPUs

00:05:05.088 --> 00:05:06.630
to meet the needs
of machine learning

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.330
and other workloads that
benefit from these specific

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:11.580
accelerators.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.978
In addition to VM families,
Google Compute Engine,

00:05:14.978 --> 00:05:16.770
as I mentioned, over
the past several years

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:18.240
has developed a
number of features

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:22.080
that provide even more value to
your virtual machine engines.

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:25.380
This includes things like
custom machine types,

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:27.900
the ability to launch
sole-tenant VMs--

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:30.490
we'll talk about it more
later in the presentation--

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:32.700
the ability to attach
various forms of storage,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:37.200
be it local SSD or persistent
disk storage of various speeds

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.310
and capabilities; and also
enhanced 100-gigabit networking

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.670
that delivers
fantastically low latency

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:46.770
and the bandwidth needed
for some of your most

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.760
demanding workloads.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.310
Now I'd like to
hand it off to Subra

00:05:51.310 --> 00:05:54.280
to give us a deeper dive on what
we mean by general-purpose VM

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:56.890
families and talk about the
workloads and the families that

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.140
tend to go with those.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.500
Subra?

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:01.330
SUBRA CHANDRAMOULI:
Hey, thanks, Jamie.

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.760
As Jamie was alluding to,
one of our core philosophies

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:07.030
at Google Cloud is to enable
our customers the flexibility

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:09.040
to choose the right
virtual machine

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.320
to run their various
different workloads.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:14.860
General-purpose workloads
make up a whole wide variety

00:06:14.860 --> 00:06:17.710
from simple web serving, to
more complex media transcode

00:06:17.710 --> 00:06:20.650
or processing types of
workloads, to data analytics,

00:06:20.650 --> 00:06:22.240
to name a few.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.270
You can also think of dev and
test as a separate workload

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:25.810
that--

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:28.600
whose requirements
are pretty unique.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.910
In addition, various
enterprise applications

00:06:30.910 --> 00:06:33.730
may be categorized under
the general purpose category

00:06:33.730 --> 00:06:35.620
as well.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.870
Our general-purpose N2
and N2D machine types

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:42.080
are a good fit for
such workloads.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.060
Our general-purpose
N2 and N2D VMs

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:46.490
are designed to support
a variety of workloads

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:50.270
by offering a perfect balance
of compute and memory resources,

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:55.330
up to 224 vCPUs with
864 gigabytes of memory.

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:58.780
N2 and N2D machine types provide
a high degree of flexibility

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:00.370
for our customers.

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:03.370
Custom machine types enable you
to rightsize your resources,

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.520
enabling great cost
saving feature.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.830
More on this later.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:10.480
They also enable
you to auto-upgrade

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.120
to new CPU generations when
you launch a new CPU platform.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:18.310
This means you can simply
choose a minimum CPU platform,

00:07:18.310 --> 00:07:20.590
and Google will
automatically run your VM

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:24.440
on the latest-generation CPU.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:27.320
And also attach up to 9
terabytes of local SSD

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:28.250
per node.

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.170
And you get high
networking performance up

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:32.510
to 100 gigabits per second.

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:36.260
These VMs are also available
globally across GCE regions

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:37.910
now.

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:40.040
The key difference
between N2 and N2D

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:43.520
are summarized in the table on
the right side of the slide.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:46.280
N2 VMs are a general-purpose
Intel processor-based,

00:07:46.280 --> 00:07:50.270
VMs supporting second-generation
Intel Xeon scalable

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.940
processor, or Cascade
Lake, and offering

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:57.180
VMs that go up to 80 vCPUs.

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.510
The N2 VMs offer
up to 20% to 30%

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:04.220
price performance
improvements compared to N1.

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:08.030
N2D is our AMD EPYC-based
general-purpose VMs

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:12.600
leveraging second-generation
AMD EPYC Rome processors.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:16.260
They come with up to
224 vCPUs per node

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:20.400
and offer you a lower price
point, with approximately 13%

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.510
lower pricing compared
to our N2 machine types.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.360
N2 VMs are ideal for
workloads that require

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.340
high per-core performance.

00:08:29.340 --> 00:08:33.000
You can see some benchmarks that
compare N2 VMs to our N1 VMs

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:34.860
on the right side of the slide.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:38.190
N2 machines are priced
at parity to N1 family,

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:40.350
enabling much higher
price performance when

00:08:40.350 --> 00:08:41.565
compared to our N1 machines.

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.470
N2D machines are ideal for
high-throughput workloads

00:08:47.470 --> 00:08:50.530
that can benefit from having
a large number of threads.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:52.840
N2D machines are
ideal for workloads

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:54.790
that can benefit from
confidential computing

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:56.140
features in addition.

00:09:00.150 --> 00:09:04.080
Switching gears a
bit, our E2 VM family

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:07.950
are ideal for applications
where cost is a priority.

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:11.760
E2 VMs offer machine
types up to 32 vCPUs

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.487
and 128 gigabytes of memory.

00:09:14.487 --> 00:09:16.820
We also offer the flexibility
of choosing custom machine

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.180
types with our E2 family.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:23.290
E2 family runs on Intel Haswell
through Cascade Lake and AMD

00:09:23.290 --> 00:09:26.840
Rome processors.

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:30.140
E2 machines are ideal for dev
and test types of workloads

00:09:30.140 --> 00:09:34.130
or for workloads that may be
batch-processed, for example.

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:37.670
This family provides up to
31% cost saving when compared

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:39.892
to our N1 family per VM.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:46.390
Switching gears again,
earlier this year in June,

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:51.640
we announced our latest VM
family under the brand of Tau.

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:55.300
The T2D is our
first family of VMs

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.790
that will be offered
under the Tau brand.

00:09:57.790 --> 00:10:00.100
Currently under
preview, Tau VMs are

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:01.780
designed to meet the
needs of customers

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.870
owning scale-out
workloads on Google Cloud.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:07.210
We chose the Tau brand
as the Greek letter

00:10:07.210 --> 00:10:11.140
tau is used to represent both
torque and the golden ratio.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:14.110
The Torque is a
metaphor that is apt,

00:10:14.110 --> 00:10:17.620
because T2D VM family allocates
an entire physical core

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:21.640
per vCPU, delivering excellent
per-thread performance,

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:26.830
while the golden
ratio tau represents

00:10:26.830 --> 00:10:29.260
T2D's focus on only
core features needed

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:31.870
for scale-out workloads in
order to deliver excellent price

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.075
performance.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.310
Focusing on T2D's
performance, we

00:10:38.310 --> 00:10:41.950
can see that it outperforms
VM types available elsewhere.

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:44.490
Here's a quick look at
two benchmarks where

00:10:44.490 --> 00:10:46.440
we showcase T2D's
price performance

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:49.380
for estimated
SPECrate and CoreMark.

00:10:49.380 --> 00:10:52.680
For estimated SPECrate,
T2D provides 42%

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.170
better price performance when
compared to other major cloud

00:10:55.170 --> 00:10:57.620
providers.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.790
Back to you, Jamie.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.130
JAMIE KINNEY: Thanks, Subra.

00:11:01.130 --> 00:11:03.960
Now we'd like to take a look
at compute-intensive workloads.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.710
And I thought we would
start by giving you

00:11:05.710 --> 00:11:09.700
an overview of what we mean by
compute-intensive workloads.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:12.190
While I've focused on scientific
and technical computing

00:11:12.190 --> 00:11:14.470
in the past, compute-intensive
workloads actually

00:11:14.470 --> 00:11:16.750
fall under a number of
verticals and industries

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:18.260
around the world.

00:11:18.260 --> 00:11:21.160
This includes workloads such
as high-performance web servers

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:25.390
that need access to
fast clock speed CPUs,

00:11:25.390 --> 00:11:28.600
as well as the networking
that allows pages to be served

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:30.520
with incredibly low latency.

00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:32.530
Also really important
for triple-A game

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.530
servers that need to
both scale up vertically

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:38.800
but also ensure that
there's no jitter that's

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:42.040
being viewed as a result of any
inconsistencies in performance

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:44.920
with the CPU or
network variability.

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:47.170
High-performance computing,
near and dear to my heart,

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:50.170
tends to fall into two
categories-- tightly coupled,

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:52.540
which will usually
rely on low latency

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:55.150
networking between a
number of machines,

00:11:55.150 --> 00:11:57.640
and also high-throughput
computing that

00:11:57.640 --> 00:12:01.870
relies on many machines working
in concert to solve a problem,

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.930
where the per-core
performance of those machines

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:09.130
is going to indicate how
well that application runs.

00:12:09.130 --> 00:12:11.350
And a great example of a
high-throughput computing

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:14.230
workload is Electronic
Design Automation, or EDA,

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.600
which needs access
to both fast cores,

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:19.390
but also a network that
makes it easy for all

00:12:19.390 --> 00:12:22.240
of those cores to
access resources

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:24.340
that are stored on a
high-performance shared file

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:26.110
system.

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:29.020
In order to meet the needs
of these unique applications,

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:31.450
we've developed
compute-optimized VMs,

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:34.390
including the C2
family, that offer

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.210
a combination of excellent
per-core performance,

00:12:37.210 --> 00:12:39.910
consistent performance,
and also isolation

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:42.460
between virtual machines.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:47.200
The C2 VMs, as we can see here
with the benchmark results that

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.380
compare C2 to the
N1 family, offer

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:54.140
better per-core performance,
offer low latency,

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:57.370
high bandwidth
networking, and are really

00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:00.770
designed to meet the needs
of these unique applications.

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.310
Another element of
the C2 family is

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.560
that the standard
machine types for C2

00:13:05.560 --> 00:13:09.010
have a lower
memory-per-core ratio,

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:12.730
as these types of applications
tend to focus on CPU

00:13:12.730 --> 00:13:14.950
needs as opposed
to taking advantage

00:13:14.950 --> 00:13:18.118
of large amounts
of memory per core.

00:13:18.118 --> 00:13:19.660
Speaking of memory
per core, I'd like

00:13:19.660 --> 00:13:23.180
to now transition over to the
memory-intensive workloads.

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:25.370
So this could include a
number of different types

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:26.190
of applications.

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:29.660
It could be in-memory
database like SAP HANA.

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:32.750
It could be in-memory
analytics, OLAP systems,

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.520
and other analytic
systems that need

00:13:34.520 --> 00:13:37.310
to keep data sets in memory
for low-latency analysis

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:40.490
and access, as well as
in-memory caching that's

00:13:40.490 --> 00:13:43.250
common components
of many distributed

00:13:43.250 --> 00:13:45.140
and cloud-native applications.

00:13:45.140 --> 00:13:48.620
Think of Redis or
ElastiCache, for example.

00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:51.470
For these workloads, the
M1 and M2 VM families

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.660
are likely to be your best fit.

00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:57.620
M1 and M2 VMs can offer up
to 12 terabytes of memory,

00:13:57.620 --> 00:14:00.212
while also offering a
large number of cores

00:14:00.212 --> 00:14:01.670
to go hand-in-hand
with that memory

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.100
so that you're able
to analyze at scale.

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:08.630
The largest M2-- ultramem
and megamem VMs, for example,

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:14.420
offer over 400 cores or 400
vCPUs per virtual machine.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:18.320
These VM families also take
advantage of the fully flexible

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:21.080
infrastructure, allowing you
to adjust the CPU and memory

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:25.990
needs provisioned to meet the
needs of your applications.

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:28.550
And we also support the
provisioning models,

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:32.000
including flex cuts,
that allow your--

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:35.030
to get discounts
when you're using

00:14:35.030 --> 00:14:37.710
all of our memory-optimized
VM shapes, both current

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:38.210
and future.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:42.730
Now I'd like to
transition us back

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:45.230
to another class of workloads.

00:14:45.230 --> 00:14:47.320
These are the accelerated
workloads that

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:49.660
need access to GPUs and TPUs.

00:14:49.660 --> 00:14:51.610
Subra, would you like
to give us an overview

00:14:51.610 --> 00:14:54.160
of these workloads and the
VM families to go with them?

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:55.202
SUBRA CHANDRAMOULI: Sure.

00:14:55.202 --> 00:14:56.625
Thanks, Jamie.

00:14:56.625 --> 00:14:58.750
There are several workloads
that can take advantage

00:14:58.750 --> 00:15:00.820
of hardware GPU acceleration.

00:15:00.820 --> 00:15:04.060
Typically, these are
data-intensive workloads.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:07.390
For instance, you may have
an AI inference or a training

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:09.130
type of a workload.

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:11.230
Some of the training
workloads could be bursty,

00:15:11.230 --> 00:15:16.030
where they have to deal with a
lot of data in a bursty mode.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:17.770
Or you can think
of a data analytics

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:19.900
where you have massive
data coming through,

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:25.030
and you have the ability to
process the data parallelly.

00:15:25.030 --> 00:15:28.540
GPUs are a great fit
for parallel processing.

00:15:28.540 --> 00:15:30.880
Other types of workloads
that can take advantage

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.000
of GPU acceleration are
high-performance computing

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:36.820
and several
video-intensive workloads.

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:39.520
Our A2 VM family
includes the latest

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:43.690
NVIDIA A100 GPUs with up
to 40 gigabytes of memory.

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:45.880
In these machine
types, we support up

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:49.630
to 96 vCPUs with 1.3
terabytes of memory.

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:52.570
These machines come in various
predefined machine types

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:56.920
with support up to
16 NVIDIA A100 GPUs,

00:15:56.920 --> 00:16:01.210
delivering up to 10
teraflops of FP16 performance

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:05.903
or 20 teraflops of int8
performance in a single VM.

00:16:05.903 --> 00:16:07.070
JAMIE KINNEY: Thanks, Subra.

00:16:07.070 --> 00:16:08.560
Really appreciate that.

00:16:08.560 --> 00:16:12.890
In addition to each of the VM
families that we've mentioned,

00:16:12.890 --> 00:16:15.280
we also have a number
of GCE features

00:16:15.280 --> 00:16:18.340
that span multiple VM
families and that are really

00:16:18.340 --> 00:16:21.293
going to be relevant to a
broad range of workloads.

00:16:21.293 --> 00:16:23.210
We just want to touch
on a few of these today.

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:26.060
And I'd like to start by talking
about custom machine types.

00:16:26.060 --> 00:16:28.060
This is a feature that's
unique to Google Cloud.

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:30.460
And it's a feature that
is giving our customers

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:31.750
significant cost savings.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:33.940
On average, they're
seeing 19% savings.

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:35.440
And in many cases,
they're seeing

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:37.330
substantially more than that.

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:39.920
Custom machine types, as you
can see in the little slide

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:41.860
or diagram in the
middle of this,

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:45.520
allows you to precisely dial
in the CPU and memory that's

00:16:45.520 --> 00:16:48.820
allocated to the VMs that you're
launching within Google Compute

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:49.900
Engine.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:53.020
This is often applied in cases
where the standard machine

00:16:53.020 --> 00:16:57.160
types that we've predefined
for ease of use and flexibility

00:16:57.160 --> 00:16:58.660
might not meet your
needs, and where

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:00.940
you need to allocate
perhaps a little bit

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:04.630
more memory or a little bit
more CPU for your workload.

00:17:04.630 --> 00:17:07.900
And by allowing you to tell
us exactly what you need,

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:10.510
we don't over-provision
those resources for you.

00:17:10.510 --> 00:17:14.200
This allows us to then reduce
the cost of Google Compute

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:17.550
Engine, and we pass those
savings back onto you.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.140
Some additional
GCE features that

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:21.152
also benefit the wide
range of workloads

00:17:21.152 --> 00:17:23.069
you might want to run
on Google Compute Engine

00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:25.109
include sole-tenant nodes.

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:27.270
This is most often
applied for applications

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:29.430
that have licensing
requirements that

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:33.180
mandate that you are the
only customer, or only user,

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:36.330
running on a given
physical server.

00:17:36.330 --> 00:17:39.690
The sole-tenant nodes, you have
access to the entire machine,

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:42.090
and you have the ability to
carve that up into whatever

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:43.860
VM shapes you need.

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:47.400
Very helpful for
applications that

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:50.250
have those specific
licensing requirements.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.650
Shielded VMs are a
feature that allow

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:55.050
you to ensure that
your workloads are

00:17:55.050 --> 00:17:57.570
running on trusted
and verified hardware,

00:17:57.570 --> 00:18:02.010
taking advantage of
the TPM UEFI firmware

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:06.330
that we're running on
GCE infrastructure.

00:18:06.330 --> 00:18:09.180
And confidential VMs
provide additional isolation

00:18:09.180 --> 00:18:12.660
and sandboxing for especially
sensitive applications.

00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:15.510
You can read more
about these in some

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:18.480
of the resources that we've
provided below, accessing both

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.870
the GCE documentation
tutorials, case

00:18:21.870 --> 00:18:24.220
studies, and other resources.

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:27.060
We've also provided some links
to additional Next sessions

00:18:27.060 --> 00:18:29.220
that you might want
to attend, as well as

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:31.142
a couple of Coursera
courses for those of you

00:18:31.142 --> 00:18:32.850
who might be new to
Google Compute Engine

00:18:32.850 --> 00:18:35.790
or simply want to expand your
familiarity and understanding

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.200
of our services.

00:18:37.200 --> 00:18:38.970
With that, I'd really
like to thank Subra

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:40.090
for joining me today.

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:42.507
And most importantly, I'd like
to thank you for joining us

00:18:42.507 --> 00:18:44.110
and for listening to this talk.

00:18:44.110 --> 00:18:46.170
I hope that it improves your
understanding of Google Compute

00:18:46.170 --> 00:18:46.650
Engine.

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:48.650
And I hope that the rest
of your Next experience

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:49.692
is a wonderful one.

00:18:49.692 --> 00:18:50.400
Have a great day.

00:18:50.400 --> 00:18:51.950
Thanks.

